From: Daniel Golle <daniel@makrotopia.org>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 11:50:04 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] [GLib] Service worker script updates are sent without cookies

https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=328830

Reviewed by NOBODY (OOPS!).

SWServer::createScriptRequest() sets the first party for cookies but
leaves the request's same-site flag unspecified. On the libsoup ports
ResourceRequest::createSoupMessage() then sets no site for cookies, so
libsoup treats the soft update fetch of the service worker script as
cross-site and attaches no SameSite=Lax or SameSite=Strict cookie. A
site that answers such a request with fresh session cookies, as Netflix
does, logs the user out on every navigation that triggers a soft update.

Mark the request same-site when the script URL and the top origin share
a registrable domain.

* Source/WebCore/workers/service/server/SWServer.cpp:
(WebCore::SWServer::createScriptRequest):
---
 Source/WebCore/workers/service/server/SWServer.cpp | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

--- a/Source/WebCore/workers/service/server/SWServer.cpp
+++ b/Source/WebCore/workers/service/server/SWServer.cpp
@@ -726,6 +726,7 @@ ResourceRequest SWServer::createScriptRe
     request.setShouldBlockThirdPartyStorage(jobData.shouldBlockThirdPartyStorage);
     request.setAllowCookies(true);
     request.setFirstPartyForCookies(topOrigin->toURL());
+    request.setIsSameSite(RegistrableDomain(url) == RegistrableDomain(jobData.topOrigin));
 
     request.setHTTPHeaderField(HTTPHeaderName::Origin, origin->toString());
     request.setHTTPReferrer(SecurityPolicy::referrerToOriginString(jobData.scriptURL));
